    "case_id": "718",
    "case_summary": "A 52-year-old male patient was hospitalized due to repeated cough for 2 months. The patient denied a history of autoimmune diseases, diabetes, hypertension, coronary disease, arrhythmia, chronic heart failure or smoking. One week after his 5th cycle of chemotherapy and immunotherapy, the patient experienced shortness of breath, loss of appetite and weight loss. The patient denied use of illegal drugs or herbs, and had no fever or cough.\n\nTemperature: 36.5°C; Respiratory rate: 20/min; Blood pressure: 156/98 mmHg; SpO2: 98%. Breathing sounded normal in both lungs, without moist or dry rales. Heart rate was 115 bpm with normal rhythm. No extra cardiac sounds or pathological murmurs were heard, and no pericardial friction. No edema in either lower limb.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * CK: 4977 U/L (Reference range 41-186 U/L)    * CK-MB: 526 U/L (Reference range 0-24 U/L)    * Cardiac troponin T (cTnT): 2230 ng/L (Reference range 0-14 ng/L)    * NT-ProBNP: Slightly elevated    * D-dimer: 0.14mg/L (Reference range 0-0.50mg/L)- Imaging Studies:    * Dynamic ECG: Normal sinus rhythm without ST-segment changes    * Coronary Artery CTA: No obvious abnormality    * Chest CT: No pulmonary infection or congestion    * Cardiac Color Ultrasound: Enlarged aorta, normal left ventricular systolic function, poor diastolic function, small amount of pericardial effusion    * Cardiac MR:       Title: CMR Findings      Description: Mild decline in cardiac function with myocardial edema in signal-enhanced T2-weighted imaging and late gadolinium-enhanced T1-weighted imaging. T2 value of interventricular septum increased to 65.5 ms, T1 value prolonged to 1297.2 ms. ECV was 37%. LVEF of 64%, stroke volume of 52 mL, cardiac output of 4.6 L/min and CI of 2.9 L/min/m2.",
